. The full RNA transcriptomics dataset produced the best clustering outcome, with the highest scores in both measures, suggesting these data reflect glioma heterogeneity. In contrast, the proteomics dataset lead to poor-quality clusters, not matching the known glioma types. This outcome indicates that the proteomics dataset used was not informative for glioma patient stratification. Interestingly, the clustering obtained for the proteomics-informed dataset, i.e., transcriptomics
S
, achieved performances comparable to those obtained from the full transcriptomics dataset. The lower input dimension massively reduced the computational complexity, making this subset of features a valuable input dataset for our analysis.
